  "query_preview": "..\n\nLongwave pattern continues to support dry and stable conditions \nacross the Great Lakes today as the Mississippi Valley ridge \nflattens eastward. This unfolds as the first in a series of \nprogressive Pacific shortwaves lift out of the Rockies and into the \nUpper Midwest. The 586 dam 500 mb ridge is progged to track up the \nOhio River and into southern Appalachia by this evening. Expect \ncontinued lower column anticyclonic influence to offer sustained \nweak subsidence with high static stability, but a confluent stream \nof milder Gulf-modified air will hold just west of southwest Lower \nMichigan. Forecast soundings highlight a dry column, with PWATs \ndropping to around 0.25 inches. Insufficient boundary-layer \nsaturation for a diurnal cumulus response, but given the upstream \nwave, high cirrus should spill in aloft with varying density. \nThermodynamically, 850 mb temperatures appear relatively steady \ntoday, if not marginally warmer than Tuesday. Daytime highs should \nreach the mid-upper 50s. Upper level clouds thicken tonight while \nlower tropospheric theta-e advection works in late tonight from the \nwest.\n\nThe speed max is expected to sharpen as it cross The UP and into \nOntario Thursday, featuring a vertically stacked area of low \npressure through 300 mb. Southeast Michigan becomes positioned \nbetween the surface low over central Ontario and high pressure over \nthe Mid-Atlantic. This leads to a tightening pressure gradient \nresulting in an uptick in prevailing winds. Tenuous stability should \nlimit the window of opportunity for lower column winds to \neffectively mix down and produce higher magnitude gusts. However, \nduring the diurnal peak, should mixing depths tap into the 3-5 kft \nAGL layer, some 30-40 knots of flow would be available for gusts in \nexcess of 30 mph. The low's extended cold front washes out over \ncentral Lower, which prevents a material wind shift toward the west \nand maintains a dry forecast. The next ridge axis works in Thursday \nnight into Friday offering a more zonal orientation aloft. The jet \nresides to the north into the weekend as the next Pacific trough \nworks through the Four Corners. The next chance for rain comes \nSunday as the embedded Pacific wave ejects across the Plains. Lots \nof moving parts to contend with given the subtropical storm along \nthe southern Atlantic coast and a meandering low moving south across \nthe Canadian Prairies that interferes with the jet pattern. Highs \nrise into the 60s during the weekend. Colder weather returns early \nnext week.",
